     1	# Workout Tracker — Product Specification
     2	
     3	> Source of truth for product behavior. Agent sessions (Claude Code, Codex) and both developers load context from this file, not from chat history. Changes to locked decisions go through both developers and are recorded in `DECISIONS.md`.
     4	
     5	## What this is
     6	
     7	A private, native iPhone workout logger for two users (the developers). It matches Strong-style fast logging, with two differentiators no mainstream tracker has:
     8	
     9	1. **Equipment-aware taxonomy** — the same exercise on different hardware is not comparable, so history and PRs anchor to equipment context.
    10	2. **Honest kg/lb handling** — units chosen per set, defaulted by context, preserved as entered, never silently conflated.
    11	
    12	v1 is for the two developers only. Schema decisions (stable UUIDs, export fidelity, CloudKit-compatible models) keep public release and sync open later.
    13	
    14	## Taxonomy
    15	
    16	Four levels:
    17	
    18	| Level | Example | Created by |
    19	|---|---|---|
    20	| Exercise | Seated Chest Press | Seeded list + user additions |
    21	| Equipment model | Life Fitness Insignia Chest Press | Seeded catalog + user additions |
    22	| Machine instance | "Chest Press #2" | User, per gym |
    23	| Gym | Gold's Gym Gangnam | User |
    24	
    25	- Seeded equipment catalog ships in-app (major manufacturers: Life Fitness, Hammer Strength, Technogym, Precor, Cybex, Matrix, Nautilus, Hoist, …; popular selectorized/plate-loaded lines; order of a few hundred models). Catalog updates ride app releases. Seeded entries have stable catalog IDs that survive updates; user-created entries live in a separate ID space (dedup deferred).
    26	- Gyms and machine instances are **always user-created**. No gym seeding.
    27	- Each catalog model links to one or more exercises. Picking a machine auto-fills its exercise; multi-exercise stations (cable, Smith) prompt.
    28	- Free weights are **equipment-type tags** (barbell / dumbbell / cable / smith / bodyweight), not machine instances.
    29	- Exercises carry a **load type**: `weighted | bodyweight | bodyweightPlus | assisted`. PR/prefill/chart logic respects direction — on assisted machines, *lowest* assistance wins.
    30	- **Strength-only v1**: one set shape (weight × reps). Cardio machines may exist in the taxonomy but are not loggable.
    31	
    32	## History, PRs, integrity
    33	
    34	- **Previous performance** uses layered fallback, each layer clearly labeled:
    35	  1. This machine
    36	  2. Same equipment model at another gym
    37	  3. The exercise on any equipment
    38	- **Prefill comes only from same-machine history.** Fallback layers are reference display, never prefilled into inputs.
    39	- **PRs**: best weight per rep count (capped at 12 reps) + estimated 1RM (Brzycki: `weight / (1.0278 − 0.0278 × reps)`), computed at all three layers (machine, model, exercise). Warmups excluded from PRs and volume.
    40	- **Log-time snapshots**: entries denormalize context (gym name, machine label, manufacturer+model, unit) when logged. Deleting a gym/machine archives it; history never orphans. Correcting a machine's model prompts: apply to past workouts or future only.
    41	
    42	## Units
    43	
    44	- Per-set unit (kg/lb). Default precedence: **machine → gym → app preference** (most specific wins).
    45	- Original `(value, unit)` preserved verbatim; normalized kg stored alongside for analytics.
    46	- Display **as entered**; a whole-view convert toggle renders converted values visibly marked (≈). Charts plot normalized values; tooltips show as-entered.
    47	- Never imply equal displayed weights on different equipment models represent equal resistance.
    48	- Dumbbell volume rule: one consistent rule across the app (decide at milestone 2; no per-platform divergence like Strong's).
    49	
    50	## Recording experience
    51	
    52	- Machine/gym optional on every set; once chosen, remembered **per gym per exercise** (`GymExerciseMemory`).
    53	- **Templates are generic** exercise lists. Starting a workout at a gym resolves each exercise to the last-used machine there.
    54	- Template drift: on finishing a modified templated workout, prompt — update template / update values only / both / keep original (suppressible).
    55	- Set types: **warmup / working / failure**.
    56	- **Rest timer**: auto-starts on set completion; per-exercise durations (separate warmup vs. working) with a global default (2:00); local notification on finish.
    57	- Speed bar (from Strong): set rows arrive prefilled from same-machine history; confirming an untouched row is **one tap**.
    58	- Active workout **auto-persists every change** — crash/force-quit loses nothing. Non-negotiable.
    59	
    60	## Technical direction
    61	
    62	- Swift + SwiftUI, **iOS 17+**, iPhone-only, local-first, fully offline.
    63	- **SwiftData** persistence; models simple; snapshots denormalized.
    64	- **CloudKit-compatible schema** (UUID ids, optional relationships, no unique constraints); v1 ships single-device; export is the backup story.
    65	- No backend, accounts, HealthKit, Watch, or third-party runtime dependencies.
    66	
    67	## Data model sketch
    68	
    69	- `Exercise`: id, name, loadType, equipmentTypeTags, isSeeded, muscle grouping (light)
    70	- `EquipmentModel`: id, manufacturer, modelName, linked exercise ids, isSeeded
    71	- `Gym`: id, name, defaultUnit?, notes
    72	- `MachineInstance`: id, gym, model?, label, defaultUnit?, archived
    73	- `WorkoutTemplate` / `TemplateItem`: ordered exercises, target sets/reps, rest durations
    74	- `Workout`: id, date, gym?, notes
    75	- `ExerciseEntry`: workout, exercise, machine?, **context snapshot**
    76	- `SetRecord`: entry, order, type, reps, weightValue, weightUnit, normalizedKg, completedAt
    77	- `GymExerciseMemory`: (gym, exercise) → last machine
    78	- PRs are derived (computed/cached), never source-of-truth records.
    79	
    80	## Strong benchmark facts we build against
    81	
    82	- Strong has **no gym/location/machine entity**; equipment is a name suffix ("(Barbell)"). Our thesis is an unserved need.
    83	- Strong CSV (import target, milestone 5): one row per set; columns `Date, Workout Name, Duration, Exercise Name, Set Order, Weight, Reps, Distance, Seconds, [Notes, Workout Notes,] RPE` (10/12-column variants). Hazards: **no unit column** (ask user at import), no workout ID (group by Date+Workout Name), set tags lost, equipment in name suffix (parse to tags), localized headers/semicolon delimiters possible, Duration as "2h 38m". Imported rows populate the exercise layer only and are flagged as imported.
    84	- Our export must include what Strong's lacks: explicit units, stable UUIDs, set types, full equipment context.
    85	
    86	## Milestones (v1)
    87	
    88	0. Project setup — Xcode project (buildable folders), docs, repo hygiene
    89	1. **UI prototype on sample data** — all key screens, no persistence; ends with explicit user review before anything else is built
    90	2. Core loop on SwiftData — schema, seeding, logging, prefill, PRs, snapshots, rest timer, continuous persistence → dogfooding starts
    91	3. CSV/JSON export (full fidelity; backup + dev-to-dev sharing)
    92	4. Progress charts (Swift Charts; normalized axes, as-entered tooltips)
    93	5. Strong CSV import
    94	6. Plate/stack calculator
    95	
    96	Catalog curation runs as a parallel content task, shipped with releases.
     1	# Decision Log
     2	
     3	Decisions made during product discovery (2026-08-08 interview). Each entry: decision, and why. Reopening a locked decision requires both developers.
     4	
     5	## Product
     6	
     7	| # | Decision | Why |
     8	|---|---|---|
     9	| D1 | Layered history fallback: this machine → same model elsewhere → exercise, labeled | Strict machine-only starts every new machine cold; exercise-first reduces the differentiator to a tag. Fallback keeps honesty *and* usefulness. |
    10	| D2 | Machine/gym optional on sets; remembered per gym per exercise | Free weights, home workouts, and lazy logging must work; friction kills fast logging. |
    11	| D3 | Seed equipment models only; gyms/machines always user-created | A worldwide gym database is impossible offline and its machine inventories are unknowable. Creating a gym takes 10 seconds, once. |
    12	| D4 | Catalog depth: major brands, popular lines (~hundreds of models) | Covers most commercial gyms; long tail via user entry; exhaustive curation is weeks of work for two people. |
    13	| D5 | Audience: the two developers only (v1) | Cuts onboarding/dedup/polish scope. Stable IDs + export keep public release open. |
    14	| D6 | Templates are generic; resolved to last-used machine per gym at workout start | One "Push Day" works at every gym; gym-pinned templates break when traveling. |
    15	| D7 | Models map to exercises; free weights are equipment tags, not machines | Machine pick auto-fills exercise (speed); multi-exercise stations prompt; barbells aren't machines. |
    16	| D8 | PRs: per-rep-count bests + est. 1RM at machine/model/exercise layers | Heaviest-set-only is meaningless for machine training; mirrors the fallback layers. |
    17	| D9 | Mixed units display as entered + marked convert toggle | Converting silently (90.7 kg for a 200 lb entry) is exactly the falseness the app rejects. |
    18	| D10 | Log-time context snapshots; archive instead of delete; model corrections prompt past-vs-future | History must survive renames, re-modeling, and deletions without silently changing meaning. |
    19	| D11 | Prefill from same-machine history only | Prefilling another machine's numbers would assert cross-machine comparability we reject. |
    20	| D12 | Set types: warmup/working/failure; warmups excluded from PRs/volume | The minimal set that keeps records honest. Drop sets/RPE/supersets deferred. |
    21	| D13 | Rest timer: auto-start on completion, per-exercise durations (warmup vs. working split), notification | Strong-standard behavior; the split is a verified Strong nicety worth matching. |
    22	| D14 | Load types: weighted / bodyweight / bodyweightPlus / assisted, direction-aware | Assisted machines invert "better" (less assistance wins); naive weight×reps produces wrong PRs. |
    23	| D15 | Strength-only v1 (no duration/distance sets) | Single set shape is the biggest scope containment available. |
    24	| D16 | v1 extras all in scope but sequenced: export → charts → Strong import → plate calc | Everything ships in v1, but a gym-usable build exists early (dogfooding from milestone 2). |
    25	| D17 | e1RM: Brzycki, 12-rep record cap | Strong parity (verified from their docs); estimates degrade past 12 reps. |
    26	| D18 | Adopt Strong's template-drift prompt (update/values-only/both/keep) | Elegant, proven solution to template drift. |
    27	
    28	## Technical
    29	
    30	| # | Decision | Why |
    31	|---|---|---|
    32	| T1 | SwiftData | First-party, SwiftUI-native, fits no-dependency rule. Weak migrations mitigated by simple models + denormalized snapshots. |
    33	| T2 | CloudKit-compatible schema; ship single-device | UUIDs/optional relationships/no unique constraints cost little now; retrofitting sync later is a painful migration. |
    34	| T3 | UI-first build order | User requirement: see and approve the screens (sample data) before logic/persistence is wired. |
    35	| T4 | Xcode buildable folders (synchronized groups) | Adding files doesn't touch project.pbxproj — kills the main two-dev merge hazard. |
    36	| T5 | Repo is the source of truth for spec/context | Any agent session (Claude Code or Codex, either dev) loads identical context from docs/. |
    37	| T6 | Cross-review over double-build | Each dev's PRs reviewed by the other's agent; Codex may independently author adversarial tests for the pure-logic core against this spec. |
    38	| T7 | Unit-default precedence: machine → gym → app preference | Most specific context wins. |
    39	
    40	## Deferred / open
    41	
    42	- Assisted-machine UX details beyond PR direction (belt weight? bodyweight input?) — milestone 2
    43	- Dumbbell volume rule — milestone 2
    44	- Export column layout / JSON schema versioning — milestone 3
    45	- Notification permission timing (on first completed set, not launch) — milestone 2
    46	- Seeded catalog curation workflow — parallel content task
    47	- Post-v1: iCloud sync, duration sets/cardio, drop sets/RPE/supersets, catalog dedup, Watch/HealthKit
     1	# Workout Tracker — Agent & Contributor Guide
     2	
     3	Private iPhone workout tracker (two developers). **Read `docs/SPEC.md` before making product-behavior changes; record decision changes in `docs/DECISIONS.md`.** Those files — not chat history — are the source of truth.
     4	
     5	## Project layout
     6	
     7	- `WorkoutTracker.xcodeproj` — Xcode project. Uses **buildable folders** (filesystem-synchronized groups): to add a source file, just create it under `WorkoutTracker/`; never edit `project.pbxproj` to register files.
     8	- `WorkoutTracker/` — app sources.
     9	  - `App/` — entry point, root navigation
    10	  - `Domain/` — value types and (later) SwiftData models, pure logic
    11	  - `Features/<Feature>/` — one folder per screen/feature (SwiftUI)
    12	  - `SampleData/` — in-memory prototype data (milestone 1; removed when SwiftData lands)
    13	- `docs/` — SPEC.md (product spec), DECISIONS.md (decision log)
    14	
    15	## Conventions
    16	
    17	- Swift 5 / SwiftUI, iOS 17+, iPhone-only, portrait. No third-party runtime dependencies.
    18	- SwiftData models: CloudKit-compatible — UUID ids, optional relationships, **no** `@Attribute(.unique)`.
    19	- Units: never convert silently. Stored weights keep `(value, unit)` as entered plus `normalizedKg`. Converted display values are marked (≈).
    20	- PR/volume logic must respect exercise `loadType` (assisted: lower is better) and exclude warmups.
    21	- Pure logic (unit math, PR computation, fallback selection) lives in `Domain/` free of UI imports, unit-tested.
    22	
    23	## Build & run
    24	
    25	```sh
    26	# Build (simulator SDK, no signing)
    27	xcodebuild -project WorkoutTracker.xcodeproj -scheme WorkoutTracker \
    28	  -sdk iphonesimulator -configuration Debug build
    29	
    30	# Prototype screenshots: launch env PROTO_SCREEN=start|active|history|gyms|exercises
    31	# preselects a screen (milestone 1 only).
    32	```
    33	
    34	## Workflow
    35	
    36	- Short-lived branches off `main`, small PRs, cross-review by the other developer's agent.
    37	- Commits/PRs must not break `xcodebuild build`.
    38	
    39	## Agent skills
    40	
    41	### Issue tracker
    42	
    43	Local markdown under `.scratch/` — no GitHub remote is configured yet. See `docs/agents/issue-tracker.md`.
    44	
    45	### Triage labels
    46	
    47	Default five canonical roles (`needs-triage`, `needs-info`, `ready-for-agent`, `ready-for-human`, `wontfix`), unchanged. See `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`.
    48	
    49	### Domain docs
    50	
    51	Single-context — `CONTEXT.md` and `docs/adr/` at the repo root (created lazily by `/domain-modeling` as terms/decisions resolve). See `docs/agents/domain.md`.
     1	# 01 — SwiftData schema & persistent store
     2	
     3	**What to build:** The app boots on a real SwiftData store instead of in-memory sample data. All entities from the SPEC data-model sketch exist as SwiftData models (Exercise, EquipmentModel, Gym, MachineInstance, WorkoutTemplate/TemplateItem, Workout, ExerciseEntry with context snapshot fields, SetRecord, GymExerciseMemory), following the CloudKit-compatibility rules: UUID ids, optional relationships, no unique constraints.
     4	
     5	**Blocked by:** None — can start immediately.
     6	
     7	**Status:** ready-for-agent
     8	
     9	- [ ] App launches with a SwiftData ModelContainer; prototype value types in `Domain/PrototypeModels.swift` are removed or reduced to enums shared with the schema (WeightUnit, SetType, LoadType, EquipmentTag)
    10	- [ ] SetRecord stores weight as entered (`weightValue`, `weightUnit`) plus `normalizedKg`
    11	- [ ] ExerciseEntry carries denormalized snapshot fields (gym name, machine label, manufacturer+model)
    12	- [ ] A save-then-fetch round-trip test passes in a test target (create workout → quit context → refetch → identical values)
    13	- [ ] No `@Attribute(.unique)` anywhere; all relationships optional
     1	# 02 — Seed exercise & equipment catalog
     2	
     3	**What to build:** On first launch the store is populated with the seeded exercise list (with load types and equipment tags) and a starter equipment-model catalog (major manufacturers, popular lines — small at first, grown alongside development). The Exercises tab shows this real seeded data. Seeded entries carry stable catalog identifiers that survive app updates; user-created entries are distinguishable from seeded ones.
     4	
     5	**Blocked by:** 01 — SwiftData schema & persistent store.
     6	
     7	**Status:** ready-for-agent
     8	
     9	- [ ] First launch seeds exercises and equipment models exactly once; relaunch does not duplicate
    10	- [ ] Each catalog model links to one or more exercises (multi-exercise stations link to several)
    11	- [ ] Seeded vs user-created is queryable (isSeeded flag or separate ID space per SPEC)
    12	- [ ] Exercises tab lists seeded exercises with load-type badges from the store, not SampleStore
     1	# 03 — Real gym & machine creation
     2	
     3	**What to build:** The "Add Gym…" and "Add Machine…" buttons (currently no-ops in the prototype) create and persist real records. Creating a machine means: label it, optionally pick a model from the catalog (searchable), optionally set a default unit. Gyms get a name, optional city, and default unit. Both survive relaunch and appear everywhere gyms/machines are listed or picked.
     4	
     5	**Blocked by:** 01, 02.
     6	
     7	**Status:** ready-for-agent
     8	
     9	- [ ] Add Gym flow creates a persisted Gym with name and default unit; appears in Gyms tab and gym picker
    10	- [ ] Add Machine flow creates a persisted MachineInstance linked to the gym, optionally to a catalog model
    11	- [ ] Machine picker in active workout lists real machines for the current gym
    12	- [ ] Users can add a missing equipment model inline (user ID space) when the catalog lacks it
     1	# 04 — Start & log a real workout (core loop)
     2	
     3	**What to build:** Starting a workout (empty or from template) creates a persisted Workout at the current gym; every set edit, completion toggle, machine choice, and unit change auto-persists immediately. Unit defaults follow machine → gym → app preference. Finishing stores the workout in history. The interruption guarantee from SPEC holds: force-quit mid-workout, relaunch, and the active workout resumes exactly as it was.
     4	
     5	**Blocked by:** 01, 02, 03.
     6	
     7	**Status:** ready-for-agent
     8	
     9	- [ ] Sets persist as entered: value + unit + normalizedKg; per-set unit toggle works against real records
    10	- [ ] Set types (warmup/working/failure) and completion state persist
    11	- [ ] Machine selection persists on the entry and updates GymExerciseMemory for that (gym, exercise)
    12	- [ ] Kill the app mid-workout → relaunch → active workout resumes with identical state
    13	- [ ] Finish moves the workout to history; Cancel discards it after confirmation
     1	# 05 — History & log-time equipment snapshots
     2	
     3	**What to build:** History and workout detail read persisted workouts. Each entry displays its log-time context snapshot (machine label, manufacturer + model, gym) rather than live references, so renaming or archiving a machine after the fact never changes what past workouts show. Deleting a gym or machine archives it; archived equipment disappears from pickers but history keeps rendering.
     4	
     5	**Blocked by:** 04.
     6	
     7	**Status:** ready-for-agent
     8	
     9	- [ ] History groups persisted workouts by month with gym + unit badge + stats, as in the prototype
    10	- [ ] Workout detail shows sets as entered (unit per set, W/F markers) and snapshot equipment labels
    11	- [ ] Rename a machine → old workouts still show the old label; new workouts show the new one
    12	- [ ] Delete a machine/gym with history → archived, not destroyed; pickers hide it, history unaffected
     1	# 06 — Same-machine prefill + layered previous performance
     2	
     3	**What to build:** Real history queries replace the sample data. When an exercise starts on a machine, set rows prefill from that machine's most recent same-index sets — one tap confirms a repeat set. The previous-performance sheet shows the three real layers: this machine, same equipment model at other gyms, and the exercise on any equipment — each labeled, with fallback layers shown as reference and never prefilled.
     4	
     5	**Blocked by:** 04, 05.
     6	
     7	**Status:** ready-for-agent
     8	
     9	- [ ] Prefill draws only from the same MachineInstance's history; a machine with no history prefills nothing
    10	- [ ] The "previous" column shows same-index sets from the last workout containing this exercise on this machine
    11	- [ ] Layer 2 finds machines sharing the same EquipmentModel at other gyms (the travel case)
    12	- [ ] Layer 3 aggregates the exercise across all equipment, labeled not-comparable
    13	- [ ] Layer selection logic lives in Domain/ free of UI imports, unit-tested
     1	# 07 — PR computation (Brzycki, load-type direction)
     2	
     3	**What to build:** Records become real: best weight per rep count (capped at 12 reps) and estimated 1RM via Brzycki, computed at all three layers (machine instance, equipment model, exercise). Load-type direction is respected — for assisted exercises the best is the *lowest* assistance — and warmup sets are excluded from all records and volume. Results appear in the previous-performance sheet.
     4	
     5	**Blocked by:** 06.
     6	
     7	**Status:** ready-for-agent
     8	
     9	- [ ] Pure functions in Domain/, unit-tested: rep-count bests, Brzycki e1RM (`weight / (1.0278 − 0.0278 × reps)`), 12-rep cap
    10	- [ ] Assisted exercises invert comparison (lower normalizedKg wins); bodyweight+added compares added weight
    11	- [ ] Warmup sets never contribute to records or volume
    12	- [ ] Mixed-unit history compares via normalizedKg but displays each record as entered
    13	- [ ] Tests cover: mixed units, assisted inversion, warmup exclusion, reps > 12 ignored for records
     1	# 08 — Rest timer: real per-exercise durations
     2	
     3	**What to build:** Rest durations stop being hardcoded. Each exercise can carry its own working-set and warmup-set rest durations, falling back to a global default (2:00 working / 1:00 warmup). Completing a set auto-starts the timer with the right duration; a local notification fires when rest ends (permission requested on first completed set, not app launch).
     4	
     5	**Blocked by:** 04.
     6	
     7	**Status:** ready-for-agent
     8	
     9	- [ ] Per-exercise warmup/working rest durations persist and are editable from the exercise's entry in the active workout
    10	- [ ] Global defaults used when no per-exercise value is set
    11	- [ ] Timer auto-starts with warmup vs working duration based on the completed set's type
    12	- [ ] Local notification on timer end; permission first requested after the first completed set
     1	# 09 — Template resolution + drift prompt
     2	
     3	**What to build:** Templates become real and gym-aware. Starting a template at a gym creates a workout whose exercises resolve to the last-used machine at that gym (via GymExerciseMemory); exercises with no memory at this gym start machineless. Finishing a workout that deviated from its template offers the four-option drift prompt: update template / update values only / update both / keep original (suppressible in settings).
     4	
     5	**Blocked by:** 03, 04.
     6	
     7	**Status:** ready-for-agent
     8	
     9	- [ ] Templates persist (name, ordered exercises, target sets/reps)
    10	- [ ] Starting "Push Day" at gym A picks gym-A machines; at gym B picks gym-B machines — same template
    11	- [ ] Drift detection: added/removed exercises or changed set counts vs the template trigger the prompt
    12	- [ ] All four prompt options behave per SPEC; a settings toggle suppresses the prompt
    13	- [ ] Save-as-template offered when finishing a from-scratch workout

